sections.content:
The mobile-first structure, verified conceptually:

```css
/* BASE = mobile (no query — the smallest screen IS the default) */
.cards { display: grid; gap: 1rem; }
.card  { padding: 1rem; }

/* Layer complexity UP as space appears (min-width, not max-width) */
@media (min-width: 48em) {          /* tablet */
  .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
}
@media (min-width: 75em) {          /* desktop */
  .cards { grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); }
  .card  { padding: 2rem; }
}

/* The query-free alternative — grid decides by space available */
.cards {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(16rem, 1fr));
  /* columns = however many fit ≥16rem; no breakpoints needed */
}

/* Fluid type — one rule, all screens */
h1 { font-size: clamp(1.5rem, 1rem + 2vw, 3rem); }  /* min, preferred, max */
```

Why mobile-first min-width (the reasoning interviewers want):
- Performance: phones download only the base; desktop adds layers — the constrained device gets the least CSS to process
- Constraint breeds clarity: designing small forces content prioritization; adding is easier than subtracting
- Fewer overrides: max-width desktop-first needs 'undo' rules as screens shrink; min-width only ADDS — no overridden properties

Choosing breakpoints (the discipline that separates juniors): resize the window and watch WHERE the design breaks — set breakpoints there, at content boundaries. Copying Bootstrap's 576/768/992/1200 verbatim for a layout that breaks at 900px is the anti-pattern.
